---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `QUARTER 1 gave you the pieces.
  input, variables, decisions, loops, lists

QUARTER 2 gave you three ways to think about them.
  ABSTRACTION   package it up and stop looking inside
  ALGORITHMS    two correct answers can cost differently
  DATA          the number is not the same as the truth`;

const Figure2 = `THE PATTERN THIS QUARTER KEPT MAKING

  2.10   a correct search, a wrong answer, no error
  2.14   the same bits, four different meanings
  2.16   every sample measured right, the shape lost
  2.19   every record accurate, the conclusion biased
  2.20   every bar true, the impression false

  Correct is not the same as right.`;

const Notation = `a ← expression           assign a COPY of the result
aList ← bList            assigns a COPY of the list
DISPLAY(expression)      show it, FOLLOWED BY A SPACE
=  ≠  >  <  ≥  ≤            comparison.  = means EQUALS

aList[1]                 THE FIRST ELEMENT
LENGTH(aList)            how many
INSERT(aList, i, value)  shift right, place at i
APPEND(aList, value)     place at the end
REMOVE(aList, i)         remove and shift left

a MOD b                  remainder
a / b                    TRUE division.  17 / 5 is 3.4
                         there is no rounding operator

REPEAT n TIMES { }       no loop counter is provided
REPEAT UNTIL(cond) { }   repeats UNTIL it becomes true
FOR EACH item IN aList { }

IF(cond) { } ELSE { }    the only two selection forms

PROCEDURE name(p1, p2)
{
  RETURN(expression)     hands back AND exits immediately
}
result ← name(a, b)`;

const T1 = `let size = 10;

function grow(size) {
  size = size * 2;
  return size;
}

console.log(grow(size));
console.log(size);`;

const T2 = `let ids = [3, 8, 15, 22, 30, 41, 56];

function find(list, target) {
  let low = 0;
  let high = list.length - 1;
  let checks = 0;

  while (low <= high) {
    let mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);
    checks = checks + 1;

    if (list[mid] === target) {
      return checks;
    }
    if (list[mid] < target) {
      low = mid + 1;
    } else {
      high = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  return -1;
}

console.log(find(ids, 22));
console.log(find(ids, 3));
console.log(find(ids, 7));`;

const T3 = `PROCEDURE label(n)
{
  IF(n MOD 2 = 0)
  {
    RETURN("even")
  }
  RETURN("odd")
}

aList ← [4, 7, 10]

FOR EACH item IN aList
{
  DISPLAY(label(item))
}`;

const T4 = `let steps = 0;
let n = 6;

for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
  for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
    steps = steps + 1;
  }
}

console.log(steps);`;

const T5 = `bList ← [2, 4, 6]
aList ← bList

aList[2] ← 99

FOR EACH item IN aList
{
  DISPLAY(item)
}
FOR EACH item IN bList
{
  DISPLAY(item)
}`;

const T6 = `function toDecimal(bits) {
  let total = 0;
  let place = 1;

  for (let i = bits.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (bits[i] === "1") {
      total = total + place;
    }
    place = place * 2;
  }

  return total;
}

console.log(toDecimal("11010"));
console.log(toDecimal("100000"));`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.22: Quarter 2 Review</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.22</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Quarter 2 Review</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What This Quarter Was</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Three Ways of Thinking" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Abstraction</h2>
              <p>A procedure with a parameter. Then you stop looking inside it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Scope is what makes that safe &mdash; inward no, outward yes.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And you built two projects on it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Algorithms</h2>
              <p>Linear search never fails. Binary search is far faster and needs sorted data.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Reasonable time is polynomial or lower. Unreasonable is exponential or factorial.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Some problems have no algorithm at all.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Data</h2>
              <p>Bits mean nothing until a program says what they are.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Fixed bits have limits: overflow, round-off, sampling.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And information is something you extract, not something you receive.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>But Here Is the Thread</h2>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Five Times, One Idea" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Correct Is Not the Same as Right</h2>
              <p>Every one of those five produced accurate output.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Every one of them could mislead somebody.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>That is the most transferable thing in the quarter</b>, and it is not on the reference sheet.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What the Exam Will Ask You</h2>
              <p><b>Algorithms and Programming: 30&ndash;35%</b> of the multiple choice.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Data: 17&ndash;22%.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Between them, roughly half the paper. That was this quarter.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today</h2>
              <p>Six traces. Then the checklist. Then the checkpoint.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Then you run your Build 2 program for everybody.</p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Notation -->
        <section id="notation">
          <h2>The Exam Notation, Assembled</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Everything you have met, on one card.</b> You get this booklet on
            exam day and a proctor collects it afterwards. Read it before the traces.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Notation}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Exam Notation — the whole of it, so far"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Three things on that card cost more marks than the rest put
            together:</b>
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <code>DISPLAY</code> emits a <b>trailing space</b>, so consecutive
              calls give space-separated output.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Lists start at 1.</b> Every index answer is one higher than the
              JavaScript equivalent.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>aList ← bList</code> assigns a <b>copy</b>. In JavaScript it
              does not, and the same program gives different answers in the two
              notations.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Traces -->
        <section id="traces">
          <h2>Warm-Up: Six Traces</h2>

          <p>
            <b>On paper. No running anything.</b> Four in JavaScript, two in exam
            notation, and the notation is not labeled in the order you would
            expect &mdash; check which one you are in before you answer.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 1 &mdash; JavaScript</h3>
          <p>What does this display?</p>
          <Code code={T1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Trace 1" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 2 &mdash; JavaScript</h3>
          <p>Three numbers. Build a table for the first call before you answer.</p>
          <Code code={T2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Trace 2" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />
          <p>
            <b>Then answer:</b> one of these is found in a single check. Say why
            that value, and not because you were lucky.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 3 &mdash; Exam Notation</h3>
          <p>What does this display? <b>Every character, including spaces.</b></p>
          <Code code={T3} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Trace 3" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />
          <p>
            <b>Then answer:</b> there is no <code>ELSE</code> and the procedure
            still always returns exactly one value. Explain why.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 4 &mdash; JavaScript</h3>
          <p>What does this display?</p>
          <Code code={T4} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Trace 4" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />
          <p>
            <b>Then answer:</b> you have seen this number this quarter, attached to
            a different algorithm on a list of the same length. Which one, and why
            is that not a coincidence?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 5 &mdash; Exam Notation</h3>
          <p>What does this display?</p>
          <Code code={T5} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Trace 5" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />
          <p>
            <b>Then answer:</b> write the same thing in JavaScript with{" "}
            <code>let aList = bList;</code> and <code>aList[1] = 99;</code>.{" "}
            <b>The answers differ.</b> Say which one the exam expects.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Trace 6 &mdash; JavaScript</h3>
          <p>What does this display?</p>
          <Code code={T6} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Trace 6" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />
          <p>
            <b>Then answer:</b> the second one is a power of 2. Say how you can
            tell from the bits alone, without adding anything up.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checklist -->
        <section id="checklist">
          <h2>Before the Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Go down this list and mark anything you cannot do without looking
            it up.</b> Whatever you mark is your revision, and there is nothing
            else on the list worth spending time on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Abstraction</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write a procedure with two parameters and a return value</li>
            <li>Predict what a shadowed parameter does to the variable outside</li>
            <li>Say why a procedure can see outward but not inward</li>
            <li>
              <b>Name a procedure in your own code that would count as
              student-developed &mdash; and one that would not</b>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Algorithms</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Trace a linear search and give its worst case for a list of n</li>
            <li>Trace a binary search and count the comparisons</li>
            <li>Say what binary search requires, and what happens if it does not have it</li>
            <li>Count the executions of a statement inside nested loops</li>
            <li>
              Say what makes a running time <b>reasonable</b> rather than
              unreasonable
            </li>
            <li>Define an undecidable problem, and a heuristic</li>
            <li>Tell a decision problem from an optimization problem</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Data</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Convert decimal to binary and back</li>
            <li>Compare and order binary numbers</li>
            <li>Say why the same byte can be a number, a letter, or a color</li>
            <li>Explain overflow, and explain round-off, and say how they differ</li>
            <li>Describe sampling, and say what too low a rate loses</li>
            <li>Calculate a compression ratio; choose lossless or lossy for a context</li>
            <li>Say what metadata is <i>for</i>, and what changing it does not change</li>
            <li>Explain why a correlation does not establish a cause</li>
            <li>
              Explain why collecting more data does not remove bias
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Notation</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Translate a <code>while</code> loop into <code>REPEAT UNTIL</code></li>
            <li>Give the trailing-space output of two consecutive <code>DISPLAY</code> calls</li>
            <li>Convert a JavaScript index answer into the exam's 1-based equivalent</li>
            <li>
              <b>State what <code>aList ← bList</code> does, and what JavaScript
              does instead</b>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>The Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Same session, straight after this. 30 minutes, on paper, closed
            notes.</b>
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><b>Exam format and exam pace.</b> Mixed notation, with no warning about which is which.</li>
            <li><b>Includes a multi-select question</b> &mdash; select all that apply, no partial credit for a partial answer.</li>
            <li>Covers the whole quarter, weighted the way the exam is: algorithms heaviest, then data.</li>
            <li><b>40 points</b>, which is just under 7% of the quarter.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Then the gallery walk.</b> Everybody runs their Build 2 program.
            Bring it working, or bring it broken and be able to say what broke.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              You wrote two projects this quarter. What did Build 2 teach you that
              Build 1 could not have?
            </li>
            <li>
              Pick one of the five items in the <i>correct is not the same as
              right</i> list. Describe a situation outside this class where the
              same trap would matter.
            </li>
            <li>
              Quarter 3 opens with the Create Task. Name the one requirement you are
              least confident about, and what you will do about it before session
              53.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your six trace answers with the follow-up questions, your marked
            checklist, and the reflection.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Quarter Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
